      "math_academy_id": "q-213233",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Which of the following digraphs represent a reflexive relation defined on the set $A={-1,0,1}?$",
      "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nPairs of the form $(x,x)$ correspond to the loops that start and finish at the same node in the directed graphs.\n\nSince $R$ must be reflexive, we require that all nodes (vertices) have a loop. Among the given options, only the following digraph has this property:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14121140/assets/67e71a9121f014683271e905db7278ee6c11eeff31e707e45d70550042e44c2d.png)\n\n \n\nNote that all nodes contain a loop.",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
